<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite widespread success in language understanding and generation, large language models (LLMs) exhibit unclear and often inconsistent behavior when faced with tasks that require probabilistic reasoning. In this work, we present the first comprehensive study of the reasoning capabilities of LLMs over explicit discrete probability distributions. Given observations from a probability distribution, we evaluate models on three carefully designed tasks—mode identification, maximum likelihood estimation, and sample generation—by prompting them to provide responses to queries about either the joint distribution or its conditionals. These tasks thus probe a range of probabilistic skills, including frequency analysis, marginalization, and generative behavior. Through comprehensive empirical evaluations, we demonstrate that there exists a clear performance gap between smaller and larger models, with the latter demonstrating stronger inference and surprising capabilities in sample generation. Furthermore, our investigations reveal notable limitations, including sensitivity to variations in the notation utilized to represent probabilistic outcomes and performance degradation of over 60% as context length increases. Together, our results provide a detailed understanding of the probabilistic reasoning abilities of LLMs and identify key directions for future improvement.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved impressive performance across a wide range of Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks, including question answering, summarization, and language understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Despite these advances, LLMs continue to exhibit notable limitations in probabilistic reasoning, as highlighted by several studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. While there is growing interest in exploring their capabilities in probabilistic inference, it remains unclear how well these models can reason over explicit probability distributions. Existing research has primarily focused on logical reasoning, numerical problem-solving, and Bayesian inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, with limited attention given to reasoning over discrete probability distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this gap, we present the first comprehensive evaluation of the reasoning capabilities of LLMs over discrete probability distributions. We develop a structured framework that prompts models with observations drawn from a probability distribution and evaluates their responses to queries targeting either the joint or conditional distributions. Our evaluation suite consists of three complementary tasks, mode identification, maximum likelihood estimation, and sample generation, that collectively probe distinct yet interconnected aspects of probabilistic reasoning. These tasks enable us to assess whether models can perceive the core characteristics of a distribution, infer underlying parameters that govern a distribution, and generate samples that reflect target distributions. By covering both joint and conditional settings, this study captures a broad range of reasoning abilities without requiring finetuning on probabilistic datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments reveal clear distinctions in the capabilities of different models, with larger models and those distilled from them significantly outperforming the smaller ones in most settings. Across all three tasks, models consistently perform better on queries about joint distributions than on conditional ones, which require deeper understanding and reasoning. Surprisingly, some models demonstrate strong sampling capabilities, generating outputs that closely align with expected distributions. We also observe that model performance varies significantly depending on how outcomes of the distributions are represented in the prompts, highlighting pertinent biases that potentially arise due to the structure of the training data utilized. In addition, models exhibit significant weaknesses in counting, particularly as the context length increases, revealing a fundamental limitation in their ability to retain and reason over extended input sequences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To summarize, we make the following contributions in this work:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are the first to present an extensive evaluation of LLMs’ abilities to reason over discrete probability distributions across a variety of settings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a diverse suite of complementary tasks— namely, mode identification, maximum likelihood estimation, and sampling—that investigate distinct aspects of probabilistic reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a systematic analysis across broad-ranging LLMs, revealing a substantial performance gap between small and large models, and uncover noteworthy sampling capabilities among certain models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we highlight key limitations of current LLMs, including sensitivity to the notation of probabilistic outcomes, difficulty with accurate counting, and performance degradation when reasoning over long-context inputs.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In-Context Learning:</span> After the discovery of in-context learning (ICL) capabilities in large language models (LLMs), there has been a surge of interest in enhancing inference in zero-shot and few-shot settings. This paradigm enables models to perform tasks by conditioning on input examples without explicit parameter updates. Foundational studies have explored the mechanisms and effectiveness of ICL in various contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. Building upon ICL, researchers have investigated methods to enable more advanced reasoning in LLMs, such as Chain-of-Thought (CoT) and Tree-of-Thoughts (ToT) frameworks that enhance problem-solving abilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Probabilistic Reasoning:</span> Despite this advances, probabilistic reasoning and handling uncertainty remain challenging for LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. Recent evaluations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> found that current models frequently violate basic probability rules such as complementarity and monotonicity, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> proposed a structured approach, ThinkSum, decomposing probabilistic reasoning into distinct retrieval and aggregation stages. However, studies of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> show that while LLMs understand probability concepts, they struggle to generate samples aligning with specified distributions. Another promising direction is Bayesian reasoning, where <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> introduced the BLInD dataset to evaluate LLMs’ ability to perform Bayesian inference over interconnected variables. Furthermore, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> showed that training LLMs on predictions of an optimal Bayesian model significantly improves models’ capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Numerical Reasoning:</span> Quantitative reasoning of LLMs is another aspect that has attracted notable attention. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> demonstrated that numerical reasoning skills could be effectively introduced into LLMs through automated data generation and targeted training. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> introduced the Program of Thoughts (PoT), where models express numerical reasoning as executable programs. Additionally, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> showed that large models can reason about distributions when guided properly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> indicated their limited ability to infer probabilities from raw data or tables without special training and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> highlighted biased and unreliable probability estimates by LLMs. Lastly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> revealed that LLMs struggle with counting tasks, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> attributed these counting difficulties to architectural constraints and tokenization issues inherent to transformer models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, despite notable progress, key challenges remain in probabilistic reasoning, numerical accuracy, Bayesian inference, and counting,
hence, addressing these gaps is essential for advancing the robustness and reliability of future LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Probabilistic Reasoning Framework</h2>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10739v1/prompt_format.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/236;" width="476" height="236"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt structure for evaluating LLMs on probabilistic reasoning tasks. Each prompt includes definitions of the random variables, observed samples or frequencies, a task specification (e.g., mode, MLE, sample generation), and output formatting instructions.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce our framework for evaluating the probabilistic reasoning capabilities of LLMs. It begins with mathematical preliminaries, followed by details of the standardized prompt format we utilized. We then formalize three structured tasks, and describe our comprehensive evaluation pipeline. Together, these components enable a thorough and systematic comparison across different language models.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Preliminaries on Probabilistic Categorical Distributions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize the probabilistic setup used in our study,
wherein we assess whether LLMs can understand discrete probability distributions by evaluating their performance across several tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(X_{1},X_{2},\dots,X_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(X_{1},X_{2},\dots,X_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> denote a tuple of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> categorical random variables,
where each <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> takes values in a finite label set <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We refer to the elements of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as the possible <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">labels</em> of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
An outcome of the joint random variable <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{1}\times\mathcal{L}_{2}\times\dots\times\mathcal{L}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{1}\times\mathcal{L}_{2}\times\dots\times\mathcal{L}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>
denote the set of all possible joint outcomes.
The size of the joint outcome space is then given by</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}|=\prod_{i=1}^{N}|\mathcal{L}_{i}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}|=\prod_{i=1}^{N}|\mathcal{L}_{i}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">To grant the LLM access to a distribution,
we provide a set of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> independent observations drawn from the joint distribution.
Let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}=\{\mathbf{x}^{(1)},\mathbf{x}^{(2)},\dots,\mathbf{x}^{(K)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}=\{\mathbf{x}^{(1)},\mathbf{x}^{(2)},\dots,\mathbf{x}^{(K)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the observed samples.
This information is incorporated into the LLM’s context using one of two following formats:
(1) by providing the empirical frequency of each possible outcome <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>,
or (2) by directly including the set of observations <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> in the prompt.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Prompt Format</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Probabilistic Reasoning Framework ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, each prompt begins with a fixed segment that defines the random variables <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1},X_{2},\dots,X_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1},X_{2},\dots,X_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, their corresponding label sets <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\mathcal{L}_{2},\dots,\mathcal{L}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\mathcal{L}_{2},\dots,\mathcal{L}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and the joint probability distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(X_{1},X_{2},\dots,X_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(X_{1},X_{2},\dots,X_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>. This is followed by observations from the joint distribution, provided either as empirical frequencies or raw samples. The prompt concludes with a task-specific query and clear instructions, explicitly discouraging code generation and specifying the expected output format. A complete list of prompts used for each task and setting is provided in the Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Task Prompts ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Task Definition and Performance Metrics</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe our evaluation suite and the corresponding metrics used to assess the capability of LLMs in understanding categorical probability distributions.
For each of the following tasks, we consider two settings: one involving queries about joint distributions, and the other involving queries about conditional distributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mode Identification:</span> Given a set of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> observations <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}=\{\mathbf{x}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{x}^{(K)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}=\{\mathbf{x}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{x}^{(K)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> provided to the model,
this task evaluates whether the model can identify the mode of the empirical distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Joint task.</span> We measure if the model can find the mode of the joint distribution i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{\text{mode}}=\argmax_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{L}}\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathds{1}{[\mathbf{x}^{(k)}=\mathbf{x}]}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>mode</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{\text{mode}}=\argmax_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{L}}\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathds{1}{[\mathbf{x}^{(k)}=\mathbf{x}]},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Conditional task.</span>
We condition on a random variable <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and evaluate the mode of a query variable <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a conditioning value <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{c}=x_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{c}=x_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{c}\in\mathcal{L}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{c}\in\mathcal{L}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we define:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{x}_{q}=\argmax_{x_{q}\in\mathcal{L}_{q}}\hat{p}(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{x}_{q}=\argmax_{x_{q}\in\mathcal{L}_{q}}\hat{p}(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical conditional distribution estimated from the
subset of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{c}=x_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{c}=x_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We report <span id="S3.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">accuracy</span> for this task,
indicating whether the model correctly identifies the most likely outcome.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE):</span> Given the observed dataset <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the model is asked to estimate the empirical distribution
over possible outcomes—either for the joint distribution or in a conditional manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Joint task:</span> The model estimates <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(X=\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(X=\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We measure the total variation distance (TVD) between the predicted empirical distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math>
and the true empirical distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\sum_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{L}}\big\lvert p(X=\mathbf{x})-\hat{p}(X=\mathbf{x})\big\rvert." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\sum_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{L}}\big\lvert p(X=\mathbf{x})-\hat{p}(X=\mathbf{x})\big\rvert.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Conditional task:</span>
Given <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{c}=x_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{c}=x_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model estimates <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{q}\in\mathcal{L}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{q}\in\mathcal{L}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We once again report the total variation distance for this task, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\sum_{x_{q}\in\mathcal{L}_{q}}\big\lvert p(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})-\hat{p}(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})\big\rvert." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\sum_{x_{q}\in\mathcal{L}_{q}}\big\lvert p(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})-\hat{p}(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})\big\rvert.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sample Generation:</span> In this task, the model is prompted to generate new samples from the joint
or conditional distribution based on the provided observations <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Unlike the previous tasks focused on estimation or selection, this task evaluates
the model’s generative capability—i.e., whether it can reproduce the empirical
distribution through sampling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Joint task:</span> The model is prompted to generate <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> samples based on the joint distribution provided in the context.
Let <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{O}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{O}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the samples generated by the LLM, and let <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> denote their empirical frequency distribution. We report the total variation distance, which is defined in this setting as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\sum_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{L}}\big\lvert p(X=\mathbf{x})-q(X=\mathbf{x})\big\rvert." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\sum_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{L}}\big\lvert p(X=\mathbf{x})-q(X=\mathbf{x})\big\rvert.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Conditional task:</span>
The model is prompted to generate <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> samples of the random variable <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned upon <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{c}=x_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{c}=x_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(X_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(X_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})</annotation></semantics></math> be the empirical frequency distribution over the samples generated by the LLM. We report the total variation distance, which is defined in this setting as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\sum_{x_{q}\in\mathcal{L}_{q}}\big\lvert p(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})-q(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})\big\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\sum_{x_{q}\in\mathcal{L}_{q}}\big\lvert p(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})-q(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})\big\rvert</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">By evaluating models on joint and conditional distributions, we examine their ability to handle probabilistic relationships in various settings. Each task focuses on a specific aspect, providing a comprehensive assessment of how well LLMs understand and process discrete probability distributions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Evaluation Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As outlined in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Prompt Format ‣ 3 Probabilistic Reasoning Framework ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, output formats are explicitly defined in the prompts to guide models toward consistent responses. Through extensive prompt engineering, task-specific formats are identified that models generally find easier to follow. When responses match the expected format, final answers are extracted using regular expressions, and the corresponding evaluation metrics are applied as mentioned above. While larger models typically follow the instructions closely, smaller models often return answers in inconsistent or alternative formats. To handle such cases, a capable model, <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.3-70B</span>, is used as an LLM judge. It receives the task description, the expected answer, and the model’s response, and determines whether the output reflects the correct answer. This approach supports a robust evaluation and helps ensure the reliability of reported results. We provide additional details regarding the usage of the LLM Judge, in the Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C LLM Judge Prompt ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> due to paucity of space.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Robustness Analysis</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we investigate pertinent factors that influence LLMs’ performance on probabilistic reasoning tasks by focusing on three key aspects: (1) how performance changes as the task difficulty increases, (2) how sensitive models are to variations in the label notation used to represent outcomes of the random variables, and (3) how model behavior differs when input prompts include direct, raw sample observations instead of empirical frequencies. These aspects expose distinct challenges for models and offer deeper insights into their strengths and weaknesses.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Scaling Complexity</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To examine how task complexity affects model performance, we vary the size of the outcome or label space <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math> for the joint distributions considered, which directly influences the difficulty of all three tasks. As shown in Equation (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Preliminaries on Probabilistic Categorical Distributions ‣ 3 Probabilistic Reasoning Framework ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) increasing either the number of random variables <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> or the number of labels <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math> for each variable causes exponential or multiplicative growth of the joint space respectively, requiring models to reason over a larger set of outcomes and their associated probabilities. In the mode identification task, this expansion increases the number of candidates the model must compare, making it harder to identify the most probable outcome. In the MLE task, a larger joint space implies that the model must estimate a greater number of probabilities, making accurate prediction more challenging. Similarly, in the sampling task, the model is expected to generate samples that reflect a more complex distributed set of outcomes, demanding stronger generalization and a better understanding of the underlying probabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Label Sensitivity</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments reveal that LLMs are highly sensitive to the choice of labels utilized in the set <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, with performance varying significantly across different label categories. Interestingly, even when the set of labels and frequency counts are fixed, simply changing which outcome is associated with which frequency can lead to noticeable shifts in model performance. This behavior likely stems from biases in the training data and the tendency of the model to interpret labels as part of the linguistic context, rather than discrete outcomes in a probabilistic space. Additionally, sensitivity to label variation differs across models, with smaller models generally showing greater variability. To quantify this effect, we design a set of experiments to measure models’ robustness to superficial changes in label notation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Estimations using Samples-in-Context Instead of Frequencies</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this setting, we explore the effect of including the raw observations <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{x}^{(1)},\mathbf{x}^{(2)},\dots,\mathbf{x}^{(K)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{x}^{(1)},\mathbf{x}^{(2)},\dots,\mathbf{x}^{(K)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> in the input prompts, rather than their empirical frequencies. By doing so, models should count the frequencies of each outcome and solve the probabilistic tasks accordingly. This change shifts the problem from simple frequency analysis to counting and subsequent processing of the counts so obtained. The longer the context, the more challenging it becomes for the model to correctly interpret the frequency of occurrences, leading to a sharp decline in performance. In the next section, we illustrate a comparison between these two approaches and highlight the weaknesses of powerful models in counting and managing a large number of samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section outlines the experimental setup used to evaluate LLMs on probabilistic reasoning tasks. It introduces the models selected for evaluation and describes key implementation details. Results are presented for the three core tasks, followed by additional studies that examine factors influencing model performance.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Model Selection</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we evaluate several instruction-finetuned LLMs, selected to represent a wide range of model sizes and capabilities. The models evaluated include <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.1-8B</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.3-70B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o-mini</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and OpenAI’s recently published model, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4.1-mini</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. These models span a broad spectrum of parameter sizes, from 7B to 70B, including specialized reasoning models, offering a comprehensive comparison of how different architectures handle various settings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Accuracy comparison on mode identification tasks across different joint sizes <math id="S5.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S5.T1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cond-mode</span> requires reasoning over conditional distributions, making it generally more challenging. While larger models—or those distilled from them—maintain robust performance across all settings, smaller models show a clear decline in accuracy as <math id="S5.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math> increases.
</span></figcaption>
<p id="S5.T1.6" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:511.7pt;height:107.6pt;vertical-align:-56.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_thead">
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
