<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Task-Specific Experimental Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents a detailed analysis of the experimental results for each task. To evaluate performance on conditional queries, one random variable <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and one of its possible outcomes <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> are randomly selected as evidence. Another variable is then chosen as the query variable <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and the model is asked to solve one of the tasks based on the conditional distribution <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(X_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(X_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})</annotation></semantics></math>. For all tasks, each model is prompted 100 times using 10 different prompts, and the outputs are parsed to extract the final answer for each query.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mode Identification:</span> In this task, models are asked to identify the mode of either the joint distribution <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(joint-mode)</span> or a conditional distribution <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(cond-mode)</span>. The <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">joint-mode</span> task requires selecting the outcome with the highest overall frequency, without additional computation. In contrast, the <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cond-mode</span> task involves identifying the most frequent outcome given a specified condition, requiring models to filter and reason over a subset of the distribution. Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Model Selection ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports model accuracy as the joint space size <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math> increases. Although the <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cond-mode</span> task involves fewer possible outputs, the added conditional reasoning makes it more challenging. Consequently, most models achieve slightly lower accuracy on <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cond-mode</span> compared to <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">joint-mode</span>. While smaller models struggle as the distribution grows, <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.3-70B</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT4.1-mini</span> maintain near-perfect accuracy across all joint sizes. The details of different distributions can be found in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Distributions Used for Each Joint Space Size ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maximum Likelihood Estimation:</span> In this task, models are asked to predict either the probability of all joint outcomes <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(joint-MLE)</span> or the conditional probabilities <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(X_{q}=x_{q}\mid X_{c}=x_{c})</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{q}\in\mathcal{L}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{q}\in\mathcal{L}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(cond-MLE)</span>. For evaluation, the TVD is computed for each prompt, and the average is reported over 100 prompts. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">joint-MLE</span> task appears to be straightforward for all models, with even smaller models achieving near-zero TVD across all joint sizes. In contrast, the <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cond-MLE</span> task is more challenging, as it requires models to derive conditional probabilities from the joint distribution. This added complexity results in consistently higher TVD values, with a clear performance gap between smaller and larger models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sample Generation:</span> This task evaluates the generative abilities of LLMs by requiring them to generate <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> samples autoregressively from either the joint distribution (<span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">joint-sampling</span>) or a conditional distribution (<span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cond-sampling</span>). We set <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}:=7\times|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}:=7\times|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure that the generated samples are sufficient to approximate the target distribution. Since it is inherently difficult to reproduce a target empirical distribution with a finite number of samples, we also compute the TVD between samples drawn from Python’s random library and the true distribution. This serves as a baseline for comparing the quality of samples generated by the models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.3 Task-Specific Experimental Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, TVD in the <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">joint-sampling</span> task increases with <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math>. Interestingly, <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.3-70B</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT4.1-mini</span> achieve TVD values even lower than those from Python’s random sampler, which includes sampling noise, suggesting that the underlying distribution of their generated samples is remarkably close to the target. In contrast, all models struggle with the <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cond-sampling</span> task, as it implicitly requires the models to first estimate the conditional probabilities and then generate samples that align with the resulting distribution. Surprisingly, even though no model reaches a satisfactory level of performance, <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen</span>, designed for reasoning tasks, achieves the best performance on this task, outperforming even the strongest models in other settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond distributional evaluation, we also assessed whether model-generated samples exhibit true independence. Using the Durbin–Watson statistic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and transition matrix entropy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, we found evidence of correlations in all models’ outputs, indicating that generations are not fully independent and are influenced by previously produced samples, consistent with the autoregressive nature of LLMs. In a related experiment, <span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4.1-mini</span> was prompted to generate one sample at a time across multiple prompts, we observed that it predominantly produced high-frequency outcomes while almost entirely avoiding rare ones. These findings highlight that current LLMs not only struggle with conditional sampling but also face challenges in generating truly independent samples. Full experimental details and results are provided in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Independence of Generated Samples ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To validate our findings on real-world data, we evaluated all six task configurations using the Mushroom dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. We focused on a subset of this dataset, resulting in a highly skewed distribution. The detailed results for each task are provided in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Evaluation on Real-world Data ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>, enabling a comprehensive comparison of model performance under realistic conditions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10739v1/Figures/sampling_comparison_improved.png" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/211;" width="476" height="211" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Average TVD across 100 prompts for <span id="S5.F2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">joint-sampling</span> (left) and <span id="S5.F2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cond-sampling</span> (right) tasks, with models queried to generate <math id="S5.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\times|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\times|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math> samples. Here, the sampling baseline is computed as the TVD between <math id="S5.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> samples generated by Python’s random function and the true distribution.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Robustness Analysis Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents the results of analyzing two key aspects: the sensitivity of models to variations in outcomes of the distributions, and the effect of providing raw samples in the prompt instead of their empirical frequencies.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.1 </span>Label Sensitivity Results</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the impact of label choice on model performance, a targeted set of experiments is conducted on three models: the two top-performing models, <span id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.3-70B</span> and <span id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4.1-mini</span>, and a smaller model, <span id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span>. All three are evaluated on the <span id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">joint-mode</span> task, which they handle reliably under standard conditions, using joint space size of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}|=54" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>54</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}|=54</annotation></semantics></math> and ten distinct label categories. These categories span a broad range of semantics, including computer science terms, human names, US states, countries, fruits, universities, majors, car brands, neurology terms, and food. By systematically varying the labels used to represent the outcomes of the random variables, the goal is to assess how surface-level changes in label representation influence model behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.4.2 Samples-in-Context Results ‣ 5.4 Robustness Analysis Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (right) presents the accuracy of each model across the different label categories. <span id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4.1-mini</span> shows strong consistency, with minimal variation in accuracy across categories, indicating a high level of robustness to changes in label sets. <span id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.3-70B</span> performs well overall but exhibits moderate sensitivity, with performance varying more noticeably across label sets. In contrast, <span id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span> is highly sensitive to label changes, suffering substantial drops in accuracy depending on the category. These findings suggest that while larger models or those distilled from them can generalize well across prompt variations, smaller models are significantly vulnerable to the way information is presented, even when the underlying task remains unchanged.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.2 </span>Samples-in-Context Results</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we investigate how model performance is affected when raw observed samples are provided in the prompt instead of their empirical frequencies. This setting is evaluated using the two best-performing models, <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.3-70B</span> and <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4.1-mini</span>, as smaller models consistently fail under these conditions. We repeat the <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">joint-mode</span> task across different joint space sizes, but rather than supplying frequency counts for each outcome, we provide the raw samples directly as mentioned. This forces the models to perform implicit counting—first identifying the frequency of each outcome from the raw data, and then determining the mode based on that.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results reveal a sharp decline in performance: both models, which had previously achieved near-perfect accuracy with explicit frequencies, struggle significantly in this setting. As the joint space size <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math> increases and the input grows longer, their ability to track counts and identify the correct outcome diminishes rapidly. This performance drop is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.4.2 Samples-in-Context Results ‣ 5.4 Robustness Analysis Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (left), which shows a near-linear decline in accuracy with increasing joint size. The findings point to a core limitation: the models’ inability to reliably perform counting over long contexts.
Among the two, <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4.1-mini</span> consistently outperforms <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.3-70B</span>, indicating greater robustness to the added complexity and input length. Further evidence from Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Effect of Sample Count in Context ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> shows that even when the joint space size is fixed at <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}|=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}|=12</annotation></semantics></math>, increasing the number of observed samples <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> results in additional accuracy degradation. This suggests that the challenge is not only tied to task complexity but also to the models’ limited capacity for reasoning over long sequences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To explore potential mitigation strategies, we conducted an additional experiment with <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4.1-mini</span> where the model was given access to a code interpreter. In this setup, the model was encouraged to write Python code to count frequencies before solving the task. This hybrid approach substantially improved performance, nearly matching the results from the frequency-provided setting. The detailed results of these experiments are presented in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Access to Code Interpreter ‣ Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10739v1/Figures/fig3.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/180;" width="476" height="180" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">(Left)<span id="S5.F3.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Accuracy comparison on the <span id="S5.F3.12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">joint-mode</span> task when models are given either raw samples or frequency counts. Accuracy sharply declines with increasing joint space size when raw samples are used, revealing the difficulty of reasoning over longer contexts without explicit frequency information. <span id="S5.F3.12.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4.1-mini</span> shows greater robustness than <span id="S5.F3.12.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.3-70B</span> in this setting. </span>(Right)<span id="S5.F3.12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Accuracy of models across ten different label categories in the <span id="S5.F3.12.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">joint-mode</span> task. <span id="S5.F3.12.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4.1-mini</span> remains consistently robust across categories and <span id="S5.F3.12.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span> exhibits significant sensitivity.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work provides the first comprehensive evaluation of large language models’ abilities to reason over discrete probability distributions. By examining a range of structured tasks including mode identification, maximum likelihood estimation, and sample generation, we observe clear differences in capability between models. Larger models or those distilled from them generally perform well across tasks, but their strengths are especially evident in sample generation, where the produced samples align more closely with the target distribution than those of a true random sampler. However, we find that performance consistently degrades when tasks are framed as conditional rather than joint distributions, revealing an ongoing challenge in conditional reasoning. Moreover, models remain sensitive to superficial changes in outcome labels and consistently fail when required to infer probabilities from raw samples instead of provided frequencies. Together, these findings highlight both the significant progress of modern LLMs in probabilistic reasoning and the fundamental challenges that must be addressed to achieve reliable reasoning under uncertainty.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study can extend in many directions, each requiring substantial time, effort, and computational resources. To maintain focus and feasibility, we limited our scope to a subset of these directions and aimed to investigate them thoroughly. One key limitation is the scale of the joint distributions used in our experiments. We limit the joint space size at 54 since smaller models struggled with longer contexts, leading to unstable and unreliable performance. Scaling to larger joint spaces would further degrade performance and reduce comparability across model sizes. Another limitation lies in our choice of distribution types. While analyzing both discrete and continuous distributions would provide a more complete picture of LLMs’ capabilities, we deliberately focused on discrete distributions. We identified a significant gap in understanding LLMs’ reasoning over discrete distributions, and we believe this gap deserves focused attention as a foundational problem that presents distinct challenges from continuous distributions. Finally, we restricted our exploration to a limited subset of distributions due to the substantial effort and time required to evaluate a broader range. Future work is needed to
explore and uncover these remaining directions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgements</h2>

